    <div class="split">
      <div class="split-label">
        <h2>The problem</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="split-body">
        <p>You spent a full career doing something real. You know this mission by heart. You’ve said it out loud a hundred times and it lands every time, because you’re the one saying it.</p>
        <p>Then a foundation asks for your website. Your nephew built a page in the spring and went quiet. You paid for a domain that sits there waiting. You have a curriculum you teach from memory and a closet full of photographs. All of it lives in your head or in your mouth, and what you need is something you can hand across a table.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WHAT I DO -->
<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="split-label">
        <h2>What I do</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="split-body">
        <p>Work arrives every Friday. Finished and explained in plain words. You look it over with your coffee. I keep going.</p>
        <p>Your organization owns all of it. Everything lands in a Google Drive with your organization’s name on it, and it stacks up week after week. After a year you have everything your organization has said, made, and figured out, in one place and in order.</p>
        <p>It stays yours. You tell me what you mean, and you correct me when I get it wrong. Your words, your judgment, your call. I run the machines.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0">Work has arrived every Friday since the spring. Slow weeks included.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

